# pacman's own hooks already create the service account, the state directories and reload the unit
# files, so this repeats none of that. Enabling a unit is the administrator's call, not a package's.

post_install() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  kgsm-api is installed but not running. Before starting it:

    1. Set real values in /etc/kgsm-api/kgsm-api.env  (it ships with the example's blanks)
    2. If this host signs people in, seed /etc/kgsm/kgsm-auth.env with the shared
       provider credentials — no package ships those, deliberately
    3. systemctl enable --now kgsm-api.service

  The first successful start creates the first administrator and prints its password once:

    /opt/kgsm-api/kgsm-api user bootstrap

  Install kgsm-web for the Control Panel UI. Without it the API serves its endpoints and no page,
  which is a supported way to run a node.

MSG
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'MSG'

  Restart to pick up the new build:

    systemctl restart kgsm-api.service

  If pacman wrote a .pacnew beside /etc/kgsm-api/kgsm-api.env, merge it — new settings are declared
  there and a key the file never declares binds to nothing.

MSG
}
